Firstmate fork: Discord bridge
==============================

A direct Discord bot channel for one owner. Owner messages reach Firstmate as
labeled untrusted input; Firstmate's final replies go to a Replies channel;
operational events go to a separate Operations channel. It uses only
upstream's documented extension points and edits no upstream file. Like
fork/README, this file is plain text so upstream's documentation-audience
check does not require it in an upstream-owned inventory.

Ported in part from the earlier core-edit implementation (origin/main
bin/fm-discord-*.sh, bin/fm-discord-gateway.py): the gateway client, the
sender's splitting and authenticated call, and owner-only authorization.
Not ported: the watcher shim and cadence edits, the outcome-store and
wake-drain taps, slash commands, OAuth setup, and the Approve/Reply decision
template.


Files
-----

  fm-discord-bridge.py      the bridge process: gateway, inbound checks, durable
                            inbox and outbox, reply/ledger/health sources, and
                            the operator commands (--help lists them).
  fm-discord-send.sh        one REST call per invocation: split, post-one, get.
  fm-discord-lib.sh         config loading, redaction, chunking, REST helper.
  fm-discord-reply-hook.sh  optional Claude Stop hook for full-length replies.
  fm-pi-reply-mirror.ts     Pi extension recording each settled MAIN reply to
                            the pi-replies spool (see "Pi replies").
  extension/                the process-event-adapter/1 package (adapter
                            "discord") that hands inbox batches to Firstmate.
  fm-discord-service        LaunchAgent launcher for a release (see "Service").
  ../bin/fm-discord-release builds, verifies, and switches releases.

Tests: fork/tests/fm-fork-discord-outbound.test.sh (including the Pi reply
cases, which drive the extension through fork/tests/discord-pi-ext-driver),
fork/tests/fm-fork-discord-inbound.test.sh,
fork/tests/fm-fork-discord-activation.test.sh (run loop, gateway through a
fake transport, full outbox, history paging, retention, cursor recovery), and
fork/tests/fm-fork-discord-service.test.sh (run lock, log rotation, releases,
the agent plist, and the launcher under a restart-on-crash supervisor).


How messages flow
-----------------

Inbound. The gateway delivers MESSAGE_CREATE. A message is accepted only when
its server is DISCORD_GUILD_ID, its channel is in DISCORD_COMMAND_CHANNEL_IDS,
its author id is DISCORD_OWNER_USER_ID, it is not from a bot, the bridge
itself, or a webhook, it is an ordinary message or reply, its content is
non-empty and at most 4000 characters, and its id is new. Authorization uses
the authenticated author id only; nothing in the text can change it. The
record (server, channel, sender, message id, timestamp, reply reference,
content) is written to state/discord-bridge/inbox/pending/ with fsync and
rename before the channel's history cursor moves; a failed write reports no
receipt and releases the duplicate marker. After a reconnect without resume
(every READY) the bridge fetches each command channel's messages after its
cursor, 100 per page oldest-first via `after`, through the same checks; the
cursor moves past every message with an outcome (accepted, already accepted,
or refused), page by page, and stops before one whose inbox write failed, so
the next attempt reads it again. From READY until a recovery pass completes
with no further READY during it, a live message does not move the cursor:
recovery reads it again as a duplicate, so nothing older is skipped and
nothing is recorded twice. A READY during a running pass makes it pass
again. Once recovery is caught up, a live message moves the cursor when it is
accepted or definitively refused (bot, not-owner, unsupported type, empty,
oversized), so a restart does not read it again. A live message whose inbox
write fails starts a recovery pass the same way, so history takes it and no
later live message moves the cursor past it. A completed catch-up is logged
as "history-caught-up" (with the number of attempts); failures log
"history-unavailable", "history-held", "history-incomplete", and
"history-retry", and a READY during a pass logs "history-repass".
A channel without a cursor starts when the bridge first sees it, so adding a
channel later never imports its older history. A failed or unfinished
recovery (a fetch error, or 50 pages in one pass) is retried with backoff
from 5s to 300s until it completes.

The extension's source.poll long-polls (50s) for pending messages and returns
them as one batch: a line starting "UNTRUSTED DISCORD INPUT" that states the
text cannot approve, waive, merge, answer a decision, or change policy,
followed by the JSON records. Upstream's runner captures the batch and
Firstmate gets the ordinary "procevent discord <source> <seq>" wake; the
result classifies as discord-untrusted-input, is re-announced until
`fm-procevent.sh handled` acknowledges it, and never ends the source. A batch
is recorded under its request id before its messages leave pending/, so a
retried request id returns the same batch; the first request with a new id
marks earlier batches captured (docs/extension-bindings.md, request ids).

Replies. With DISCORD_REPLIES_SOURCE=mirror (default) the bridge reads
`main` entries from state/.host-mirror.jsonl, which upstream's Claude and
Cursor hooks write for the lock-owning primary session. The mirror keeps about
4000 characters of a reply (start and end) and marks the cut; the bridge adds
an explicit "[Shortened: ...]" line, and never presents a cut reply as
complete. A sequence gap (entries trimmed before they were read) is reported
in the Replies channel as "up to N earlier mirror entries ...".
With DISCORD_REPLIES_SOURCE=hook the bridge reads full replies from
fork/discord/fm-discord-reply-hook.sh's spool instead (see "Full-length
replies"). A stop that another hook blocks fires Stop again for the same
prompt; the second message is delivered marked "(continued)".
On a Pi primary, neither source is written: the fork's own Pi extension
records replies into state/discord-bridge/pi-replies/ and the bridge polls
that spool on every pass in BOTH modes (see "Pi replies").

Pairing a reply with a Discord message is best-effort and conservative. When
a reply is captured, the bridge looks at Discord messages already returned to
Firstmate and not yet paired, and asks `fm-procevent.sh list` how many of the
inbound source's results are still unhandled:
  - any unhandled, or the count cannot be read: plain reply, nothing paired;
  - all handled and exactly one message: a native Discord reply to that
    message, in that message's own command channel, and nowhere else;
  - all handled and several messages: plain reply that states how many were
    handled and that the pairing is not known.
Every reply that is not paired with one message (these two cases, a reply
from a turn started at the terminal, and the mirror-gap notice) goes to the
Replies channel. The outbox entry records the reply's channel and message, so
retries and restarts keep them. A reply is posted only to a channel that is
still in DISCORD_COMMAND_CHANNEL_IDS when it is sent; one queued for a channel
removed since moves to failed/ with "channel no longer configured". A
continuation follows the pairing of the stop it continues. The bot's own
replies reach the gateway in the command channel and are refused as "bot".
Upstream records no link between a turn and the wake that started it, so an
exact mapping is not available.

Operations. The bridge follows state/fleet-ledger.jsonl (docs/fleet-ledger.md;
enable it with an empty config/fleet-ledger) and posts:
  task.status needs-decision   "Decision needed - <task>: <text>"   mentions owner
  task.status blocked          "Blocked - <task>: <text>"           mentions owner
  task.status failed           "Failed - <task>: <text>"            mentions owner
  task.status done             "Done - <task>: <text>"
  task.pr_ready                "PR ready for review - <task>: <url>"
  task.merged                  "Merged - <task>: <url>" / "Merged locally - <task>"
Attention messages add "Notification only: a Discord message cannot approve
or answer this." Other states and events are ignored, as are malformed lines,
other record versions, and non-https PR links. The ledger repeats records
after an interrupted capture and has no sequence numbers, so a record whose
event, task, state, key, text, pr, and via all match one seen in the last 10
minutes is treated as the same event.
Rate limit: at most DISCORD_OPS_RATE_MAX ledger notices (default 10) are
posted per DISCORD_OPS_RATE_WINDOW_SECS (default 600). A notice over the limit
is not posted on its own; it is logged as "ledger-rate-limited" (key and task,
never text) and counted. When the window ends, one summary posts instead:
"Discord notices paused: N more operational notices in <window> were not
posted one by one ... Tasks: <up to 20 task ids>", mentioning the owner when
any held-back notice needed attention. While that summary waits for room in a
full outbox, new notices join it. The window and counts are kept in the
ledger cursor, so a restart neither resets the limit nor loses the summary.
Health notices and the bridge's own delivery-failure notices are not counted;
they are already limited to one notice per episode (watcher, gateway, full
backlog) or one per failed message.
Health: when state/.last-watcher-beat (touched by every watcher loop) is older
than DISCORD_WATCHER_STALE_SECS (default 180), one "Monitoring stopped"
notice, a reminder every 6 hours while it lasts, and one "Monitoring resumed"
notice. A gateway disconnect longer than 5 minutes gives one notice and one
"restored" notice. A gateway connection says nothing about Firstmate itself.


Delivery guarantees
-------------------

Every outgoing message is written to state/discord-bridge/outbox/<replies|
operations>/ before any network call, and a source cursor moves only after
its events are in the outbox. Each destination is sent first-in first-out,
one chunk at a time; a head entry waiting to retry holds the ones behind it,
so order is kept. Long text is split under Discord's 2000-unit limit at
paragraph, then line, then word boundaries, with " (k/n)" markers; every
chunk of a paired reply references its message, and only the first chunk
carries a mention. Replies share one first-in first-out queue whichever
channel they go to. Mentions are suppressed
except the owner on attention notices; replies never ping.

  429                  wait the returned Retry-After, then retry
  5xx, network, none   retry with exponential backoff (2^n s, max 300s, +-20%)
  other 4xx            stop retrying; move to state/discord-bridge/failed/,
                       report "could not be delivered (http <code>)" in
                       Operations (without the body); later messages continue
  DISCORD_MAX_ATTEMPTS failed attempts in a row (default 30, about two hours
                       with the 300s cap; 429s count too): treated as a
                       permanent failure, reported as "gave up after N
                       attempts, last <result>"

A failed Operations message is only logged ("outbox-permanent-failure"); it is
never announced in Operations, so a broken Operations channel cannot produce
notices about its own failures.

An entry is marked sent only after Discord's success response, and each
chunk carries a fixed nonce with enforce_nonce. Remaining duplicate window: if
the process dies after Discord accepted a chunk but before the bridge recorded
it, that chunk is sent again after restart with the same nonce; Discord drops
the repeat only within its short nonce window (minutes), so a restart after
that window can show the chunk twice. Exactly-once delivery is not claimed.

The outbox holds at most DISCORD_OUTBOX_MAX entries per destination (default
500). When full, the bridge stops reading that source (nothing is discarded)
until entries drain; a mirror entry trimmed meanwhile is then reported as a
gap. Each full episode is logged once ("outbox-full", with the count, never
text), a full Replies backlog also posts one Operations notice, and the end
of the episode is logged ("outbox-capacity-restored").

Retention. Hourly while running (and at start), cleanup removes inbound
duplicate markers, delivered-event markers, and captured or claimed inbox
copies whose files are older than 30 days, and logs {"event": "prune",
"removed": n, "errors": n}; a record it cannot remove stays and is counted.
The inbox, outbox, failed/, uncaptured batches, and cursors are never removed
by age; failed/ is kept until removed by hand. Removing a 30-day-old
duplicate marker cannot readmit its message: history is read only after each
channel's cursor, and the gateway replays only events from the current
session.


Setup
-----

1. Discord application: create a bot, copy its token, and invite it to the
   server with Send Messages and Read Message History. Enable the privileged
   Message Content intent in the Developer Portal and set
   DISCORD_MESSAGE_CONTENT=1, or the gateway delivers empty content for
   messages that do not mention the bot (those are refused as empty).
2. Channels: a command channel (inbound; paired replies answer there), a
   Firstmate Replies channel (every other reply), and a Firstmate Operations
   channel. Replies and Operations must differ, and Operations never accepts
   commands. The bot needs View Channel, Send Messages, and Read Message
   History in each command channel. DISCORD_COMMAND_CHANNEL_IDS is the whole
   inbound allowlist; any other channel is refused, so a test channel left
   out of it needs no setting.
3. $FM_HOME/.env (gitignored; the environment wins over it):
     DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN=...
     DISCORD_GUILD_ID=<server id>
     DISCORD_OWNER_USER_ID=<owner user id>
     DISCORD_COMMAND_CHANNEL_IDS=<id>[,<id>...]
     DISCORD_REPLIES_CHANNEL_ID=<id>
     DISCORD_OPERATIONS_CHANNEL_ID=<id>
     DISCORD_MESSAGE_CONTENT=1
   Optional: DISCORD_REPLIES_SOURCE=mirror|hook, DISCORD_INBOUND_SOURCE_ID
   (default discord-inbound), DISCORD_WATCHER_STALE_SECS, DISCORD_OUTBOX_MAX,
   DISCORD_OPS_RATE_MAX, DISCORD_OPS_RATE_WINDOW_SECS, DISCORD_MAX_ATTEMPTS.
   A missing, zero, negative, or non-numeric value uses the default.
   Check with: fork/discord/fm-discord-bridge.py check-config
   The bridge refuses to start when any required value is absent or invalid,
   and when a configured channel is not in the configured server.
4. Fleet ledger: touch config/fleet-ledger.
5. Run the bridge: fork/discord/fm-discord-bridge.py run
   Its first start creates state/discord-bridge/, which the extension needs.
   It runs in the foreground; to run it as a login service instead, see
   "Service". `status` and `stop` report and stop it, deciding by the run
   lock (state/discord-bridge/bridge.lock), never by the pid in that file
   alone; a second `run` exits "already running". A stopped bridge keeps its
   inbox and outbox, and resumes them on start. Messages it accepts wait in
   the inbox until step 6 is done.
6. Inbound extension (outside every Git project, as upstream requires), with
   FM_HOME exported as this home:
     mkdir -p "$HOME/.local/share/firstmate-packages"
     cp -R fork/discord/extension "$HOME/.local/share/firstmate-packages/discord-inbound"
     bin/fm-extension.sh bind "$HOME/.local/share/firstmate-packages/discord-inbound" \
       --adapter discord --trust-same-user-code --consent artifact-references
     bin/fm-procevent.sh register-extension discord discord-inbound \
       --config-ref "dir:$FM_HOME/state/discord-bridge"
   Keep the printed owner token; `fm-procevent.sh retire discord-inbound
   --if-owner <token>` removes the registration. A registered source keeps
   Firstmate's supervision running, and the watcher restarts it every loop.
   To change the package, retire the registration and then the binding
   (bin/fm-extension.sh retire-binding), and bind the new copy.
   Registered before the bridge's first start, the source reports
   "extension-unavailable" results until state/discord-bridge/ exists. To
   recover, start the bridge, mark each such result handled
   (`fm-procevent.sh handled discord-inbound <sequence>`; `fm-procevent.sh
   list` shows the pending count), and run `fm-procevent.sh ensure-listening
   discord-inbound`.


Service (prepared, not activated)
---------------------------------

A per-user LaunchAgent, org.firstmate.fork.discord-bridge, can run the bridge
at login from an immutable release. These pieces exist in the repository;
nothing loads them. Installing is a separate, deliberate step.

Releases. fork/bin/fm-discord-release builds content-addressed, read-only
releases under a deployment root (default used below:
D=~/.local/share/firstmate-discord-bridge):
  fork/bin/fm-discord-release build  "$PWD" "$D"    prints the new id
  fork/bin/fm-discord-release verify "$D" <id>     manifest, read-only, no links,
                                                   no .env/state/caches
  fork/bin/fm-discord-release switch "$D" <id>     atomically repoint current
  fork/bin/fm-discord-release list   "$D"
A release holds bin/ exported from HEAD (build refuses if bin/ differs from
HEAD, untracked files included) and this directory's runtime files;
MANIFEST.sha256 lists every file and the id is its digest. build never
changes `current`, never deletes a release, and touches no runtime state.

Agent. fork/discord/fm-discord-service is the launcher: it clears the
environment, sets PATH=/usr/bin:/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin, umask 077, FM_HOME,
and quiet stderr, and runs /opt/homebrew/bin/python3 -B on its own release,
resolved physically, so switching `current` never changes a running bridge.
The bridge reads its Discord settings from $FM_HOME/.env (keep it 0600).
`fm-discord-bridge.py launchd-plist [--deploy "$D"]` prints the agent:
RunAtLoad; KeepAlive {SuccessfulExit: false}, so a crash or signal is
restarted (at most every 60s) and a clean stop is not; ExitTimeOut 75, so a
send in flight finishes; Umask 077; output to
$FM_HOME/state/discord-bridge/launchd.log (crashes only; the structured log
is bridge.log, rotated at about 5 MB to bridge.log.1). Write it outside
~/Library/LaunchAgents until installing: a plist there loads at next login.

Installing (only when authorized; never alongside a foreground bridge or
the legacy dev.firstmate.discord-gateway, which must stay disabled):
  fork/discord/fm-discord-bridge.py stop          end the foreground bridge
  fork/discord/fm-discord-bridge.py status        must say "not running"
  fork/discord/fm-discord-bridge.py launchd-plist --deploy "$D" \
    > ~/Library/LaunchAgents/org.firstmate.fork.discord-bridge.plist
  plutil -lint ~/Library/LaunchAgents/org.firstmate.fork.discord-bridge.plist
  launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/org.firstmate.fork.discord-bridge.plist
Operating:
  launchctl print gui/$(id -u)/org.firstmate.fork.discord-bridge    state, pid
  fork/discord/fm-discord-bridge.py status                          lock holder, queues
  tail state/discord-bridge/bridge.log                              events
  launchctl kickstart -k gui/$(id -u)/org.firstmate.fork.discord-bridge   restart
  launchctl bootout gui/$(id -u)/org.firstmate.fork.discord-bridge        stop
`fm-discord-bridge.py stop` under launchd also stays stopped (clean exit),
but launchd still holds the job; prefer bootout.
Updating: build, verify, switch, then kickstart -k. Rolling back: switch to
the earlier id, then kickstart -k. State under state/discord-bridge/ is
shared by all releases and is never removed by either.
After any start, check: one bridge process (launchctl's pid = the lock
holder), "gateway-ready" then "history-caught-up" in bridge.log, cursors and
queues as before, nothing new in failed/.
Not verified until activation: launchd's own restart, timeout, and login
behavior (the repository tests use a supervisor with the same exit-code
policy), and behavior across sleep.


Full-length replies (optional)
------------------------------

The reply hook was checked against Claude Code 2.1.308 in a throwaway project
(not in CI): a Stop hook in .claude/settings.local.json runs alongside the
project's Stop hooks; both receive the same payload, including the complete
last_assistant_message (6392 characters, identical to the turn's result); it
fires once per stop, after tool calls, not for intermediate messages; a local
hook that exits 1, is killed, or times out does not stop the project hook or
the turn; a stop blocked by another hook fires Stop again for the same
prompt_id with stop_hook_active true.

  fork/discord/fm-discord-bridge.py install-hook     add the hook to
                                                     .claude/settings.local.json
  fork/discord/fm-discord-bridge.py uninstall-hook   remove only that hook
Then set DISCORD_REPLIES_SOURCE=hook and restart the bridge. install-hook keeps
every existing setting, saves the previous file as
settings.local.json.before-discord-hook, is idempotent, and refuses a
symlinked or invalid file. In this checkout .claude/settings.local.json is
not gitignored, so it shows as untracked; keep it out of commits (for
example with .git/info/exclude). The hook records only Stop events of this
home's lock-owning primary session in a genuine primary checkout, always
exits 0, prints nothing, and is inert unless the bridge is in hook mode.
Claude only; a Cursor primary uses the mirror, and a Pi primary uses the
extension below.


Pi replies
----------

When the primary firstmate session runs on Pi, MAIN's chat replies reach
Discord through fork/discord/fm-pi-reply-mirror.ts, a fork-owned Pi
extension. It writes each settled MAIN reply - with no filtering, including
replies to operational wakes and background notifications (captain decision
2026-10-28: parity with Claude) - as one file in
state/discord-bridge/pi-replies/, in the SAME firstmate.discord-reply-event.v1
schema the Claude Stop hook spool uses. It never appends to
state/.host-mirror.jsonl, never captures prompts, and adds no configuration
key. The bridge polls pi-replies/ on every tick UNCONDITIONALLY, in both
DISCORD_REPLIES_SOURCE modes, through poll_pi_spool, which shares the hook
spool's validation, pairing (correlate), durable outbox, deduplication, retry,
and failure handling; events are identified as pi-<session-hash>-<seq> so they
never collide with hook- or mirror- ids. A spool file leaves the directory
only after the outbox durably holds its event, so a crash replays instead of
dropping, and the durable outbox suppresses the replay. Invalid files move to
failed/ with a pi-spool- prefix, as the hook spool does.

The extension is loaded EXPLICITLY. The operator-owned ~/.local/bin/fm
launcher (a personal script, not in this repository) must pass the extension
on Pi launches; after this fork lands, that launcher's final line becomes:

  exec pi -e "$home/fork/discord/fm-pi-reply-mirror.ts" "$@"

Nothing here relies on the global ~/.pi/agent/extensions auto-discovery, and
no file is added under the upstream-owned tracked .pi/extensions/. Loading is
the operator's step: the repository change alone activates nothing.

Interception is Pi's public agent_settled event: the only run boundary after
which no retry, compaction, or queued continuation remains. The settled final
reply is the last assistant transcript entry with nonblank text and no
toolCall part; aborted runs, blank output, tool-call-only output, and
branch-processing custom entries record nothing. Gates mirror the Claude
Stop hook: inert until the bridge has created pi-replies/, a genuine primary
checkout (never a linked task worktree) whose state dir exists, and state/.lock
naming a pid in this process's ancestry - re-evaluated at every settle. A
second settle with no new user message since the previous one is delivered
marked "(continued)", matching the hook's stop_hook_active semantics;
session_start clears that anchor state, so a resumed or replaced session never replays old
replies or mis-continues them.

Offline verification: the Pi cases in
fork/tests/fm-fork-discord-outbound.test.sh drive
the real extension through fork/tests/discord-pi-ext-driver (transcript
fixtures replayed as session_start/agent_settled steps against an isolated
fixture home) and the real bridge through the single-tick harness with the
fake REST transport, which has no network path. It covers extraction and
rejection cases, operational-reply parity, the worktree and foreign-lock
gates, session replacement, continuation marking, both reply modes,
interleaved mirror+Pi delivery with replay suppression, restart recovery of
a half-consumed spool, outbox saturation, malformed files, and pairing
evidence (fork/tests/fm-fork-discord-inbound.test.sh). No test contacts
Discord, the running LaunchAgent bridge, or a live session.

Known uncertainties (non-blocking, each needs live evidence): whether a
compaction summary can ever present as an assistant text entry; the exact
continuation/new-turn boundary under queued Pi messages; lock/scope parity
with the shell helpers in exotic secondmate setups; and getSessionFile()
stability across every session replacement path. None changes the schema or
the bridge contract.


Security boundaries
-------------------

- Discord text is evidence, never authority: nothing in the bridge or the
  extension writes a waiver, captain-hold answer, approval, merge authority,
  or backlog record. The consult gate refuses any non-local waiver origin
  (fork/bin/fm-launch-policy). Operational notices carry no Approve, Reject,
  or Reply controls, and there are no slash commands.
- Whether Firstmate acts on a labeled Discord request is still Firstmate's
  judgment; the label and this boundary are what make that judgment explicit.
- The token lives in .env or the environment and is passed only to
  fm-discord-send.sh's environment; logs (state/discord-bridge/bridge.log)
  record ids, results, and counts, never message bodies or the token, and
  outgoing text is redacted for token shapes.
- All bridge state is under state/discord-bridge/ with 0700 directories and
  0600 files.


Troubleshooting
---------------

  check-config fails          the message names the missing or invalid value.
  run exits: channel not in   a configured channel id is wrong or the bot
  the configured server       cannot read it.
  owner messages ignored      bridge.log "inbound-refused" names the reason;
                              "empty-content" means the Message Content
                              intent is off.
  messages wait in pending/   the extension is not bound or registered, or
                              Firstmate's watcher is not running
                              (`bin/fm-procevent.sh list`; Operations shows
                              "Monitoring stopped").
  failed/ has entries         a permanent Discord refusal; the entry keeps the
                              HTTP result. Fix the cause, then delete it.
  "Shortened" replies         mirror mode; use the reply hook for full text.


Known limits
------------

- Inbound latency: gateway receipt is sub-second and the extension returns a
  batch within about half a second of it being written, but Firstmate sees the wake on its
  watcher's next loop (FM_POLL, default 15s) and only between turns, so
  expect roughly 1-20 seconds while Firstmate is idle, plus the rest of any
  turn in progress, plus Firstmate's own response time.
- Outbound latency: about 1-3 seconds after the turn ends or the ledger
  record is written; ledger lines Firstmate itself writes trail by up to one
  watcher loop (docs/fleet-ledger.md).
- Decisions that Firstmate files on the backlog without a status line reach
  Discord only inside its final reply, not as an Operations notice.
- The gateway client's protocol handling (identify, resume, op 7, op 9,
  backoff, recovery after READY) is tested through a fake transport
  (fork/tests/discord-gateway-harness); the websocket framing, TLS, heartbeat
  timing, and Discord's real session behavior need a live server to verify.
- Pairing replies with Discord messages is heuristic, as described above.
- The extension's bound copy is a snapshot of fork/discord/extension; a
  change there takes effect only after retiring and re-binding (Setup step 6).
- A message in a thread under a command channel has the thread's own channel
  id, so it is refused as wrong-channel; post directly in the channel.
- Recovery limits. By design: an inbox write that keeps failing stalls
  recovery at that message (retried 5s, 10s, ... up to 300s apart, logged
  "history-held" each time); live messages are still recorded meanwhile but
  no cursor moves until the write succeeds. By design: a message Discord's
  history returns malformed is treated as an outcome and passed, so it cannot
  block its channel forever. Residual risk: duplicate markers are kept 30
  days, so a cursor held back longer than that could readmit a message whose
  marker was cleaned up. Residual risk: if the recovery thread itself crashes,
  history stays not caught up (live messages recorded, cursors held) until the
  next READY starts recovery again; a restart also does.


Task threads (design only; not implemented)
-------------------------------------------

Replies are native replies in the command channel today. Threads could be
added later, off by default behind one new setting, without changing the
inbound checks or the outbox:

- Creating: when a paired reply is queued, its outbox entry gains a first
  step "open the thread for message M" (POST /channels/<channel>/messages/
  <M>/threads), recorded before the call. A thread started from a message
  has that message's id, so a retry after a crash, or Discord's "already
  has a thread" answer, resolves to the same thread without a nonce. The
  reply then goes to channel M.
- Allowlist: the bridge keeps a durable table of threads it opened (thread
  id, parent channel, triggering message). A message in a thread is
  accepted only when its thread is in that table and the parent is still in
  DISCORD_COMMAND_CHANNEL_IDS; every other check (server, owner, bot,
  webhook, type, size, duplicate) is unchanged. Threads others open stay
  refused, so the allowlist never widens by itself.
- Follow-ups: a reply paired with a message in a known thread goes to that
  thread; Config.channel_for accepts table threads whose parent is allowed.
- Recovery: each known, unarchived thread gets its own history cursor, read
  with the same oldest-first `after` paging after a reconnect.
- Archived and locked threads: sending to an archived thread reopens it when
  the bot may send in threads; a locked or deleted thread is a permanent
  failure (failed/ plus an Operations notice), never a silent fallback.
- Permissions: Create Public Threads and Send Messages in Threads in each
  command channel; startup would check them before enabling the setting.
